tel 1.0

base praxinoscope
import soundness

info   an RE2 regular-expression engine: linear-time matching, and decidable containment of patterns
detail
    _Praxinoscope_ is a pure-Scala implementation of [RE2](https://github.com/google/re2/wiki/Syntax)
    syntax: a regular-expression engine whose matching time is linear in the length of the
    input, whatever the pattern. It achieves that by compiling a pattern to a finite automaton
    and simulating it without backtracking, which is possible because the RE2 subset leaves
    out the constructs an automaton cannot express — backreferences, lookahead and lookbehind,
    possessive quantifiers and atomic groups — and rejects them when a pattern is parsed. The
    same restriction makes two further questions decidable: whether one pattern matches
    everything another does (`subsumes`) and whether any input matches both (`intersects`).

    A pattern is parsed into a `Motif`, which holds its structural `Motif.Node` tree, its
    count of capturing groups and its compiled `Program`, and is matched by `matches`, `seek`,
    `search`, `groups` and `spans`. Character classes are sets of codepoints, `Ranges`;
    the `Pike` virtual machine runs a program over any input a `Symbolizer` can decode into
    symbols (`Text` as Unicode codepoints, today); and `Subsumption` decides containment and
    intersection over compiled programs.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val motif: Motif = Motif.parse(t"[a-z]+@[a-z]+\\.com")
    motif.matches(t"jon@example.com")       // true
    motif.seek(t"mail jon@example.com")     // the interval of the match
    motif.subsumes(Motif.parse(t"jon@example\\.com"))   // true

    Motif.parse(t"(?:a+)+b").matches(("a".repeat(200).nn).tt)      // false, in linear time
    Motif.parse(t"(a)\\1")                               // raises Motif.Error: a backreference
    ```

    The usual way to reach the engine is not through `Motif` but through kaleidoscope's
    `r"…"` literals, with `import regexBackends.re2`: every literal in scope is then parsed
    and compiled as the code compiles, rejected there if it leaves the RE2 subset, and staged
    into its extractor so that nothing is parsed at runtime, while a captureless literal is
    further compiled to a static automaton, `Fsa`. The marker type `Re2` is what tags such a
    `Regex in Re2`; its `Regex.Engine` instance lives in kaleidoscope, which is above this
    library.

    The syntax accepted is RE2's: literals and `.`; the escapes `\n \t \r \f \a \v \e`,
    `\xHH`, `\x{…}` and escaped punctuation; the ASCII classes `\d \w \s` and their
    complements; POSIX classes inside a bracket, `[[:alpha:]]` and `[[:^alpha:]]`; Unicode
    general categories and scripts, `\pL`, `\p{Lu}`, `\p{Greek}`, `\P{…}` and `\p{^…}`;
    bracketed classes with ranges and negation; quoted runs `\Q…\E`; alternation; capturing,
    non-capturing `(?:…)` and named `(?<name>…)`/`(?P<name>…)` groups (a name plays no part,
    the group is numbered positionally); the quantifiers `* + ? {n} {n,} {n,m}` with a
    reluctant `?` suffix, a repetition count at most `Motif.maxRepetition` (1000); the inline
    flags `i` (case folding), `m` (multi-line anchors), `s` (dot matches newline) and `U`
    (swap greediness), as `(?i)` to the end of the enclosing group or `(?i:…)` for one group,
    and `(?-i)` to clear; and the anchors `^ $ \A \z \b \B`. Matching is over Unicode
    codepoints, so an astral character is one symbol.

    The library is a single source set, `praxinoscope.core`, which compiles for the JVM,
    Scala.js and Scala Native, and is not capture-checked: the Pike VM is mutable by design.
    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer praxinoscope.Motif
refer praxinoscope.Re2
refer praxinoscope.Program
refer praxinoscope.Subsumption
refer kaleidoscope.Regex
refer kaleidoscope.regexBackends

entry .Re2   the RE2 engine's marker: the `Form` of a kaleidoscope `Regex in Re2`
    A sealed phantom trait with no members. A kaleidoscope `Regex in Re2` is a regular
    expression tagged to be matched by this library: it is the type of an `r"…"` literal
    compiled under `import regexBackends.re2`, and of `Regex#re2`, which retags a regex
    after checking it against the RE2 subset. The `Regex.Engine` instance for `Re2` cannot
    live in this trait's companion, since `Regex` is defined above this library, so it is
    `Regex.Engine.re2` in kaleidoscope — still in implicit scope, so no import is needed to
    match a `Regex in Re2`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import regexBackends.re2

    val regex: Regex in Re2 = r"ab?c"
    regex.matches(t"ac")   // true
    ```

entry .Motif   a parsed RE2 regular expression: its pattern, its node tree, its captures and its program
  detail
      A `Motif` is the result of parsing a pattern in RE2 syntax: the source `#pattern`, the
      structural `#node` tree, the number of capturing groups `#captures`, and the compiled
      `#program` the matcher runs. `.parse` (or `.apply`) builds one, raising `Motif.Error`
      with the position and reason for anything the syntax rejects, including every construct
      RE2 deliberately leaves out.

      Matching is always linear in the input: `#matches` is a whole-input test, `#seek` finds
      the leftmost match from a position, `#search` every match in turn, and `#groups`,
      `#spans` and `#slots` report what each capturing group matched. `#subsumes` and
      `#intersects` compare the languages of two motifs, which only an automaton engine can
      decide.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val motif = Motif.parse(t"(a+)(b+)")
      motif.matches(t"aabb")          // true
      motif.groups(t"aabb")           // List(Interval.zerary(0, 2), Interval.zerary(2, 4))
      motif.search(t"ab aabb")        // Chain(Interval.zerary(0, 2), Interval.zerary(3, 7))
      Motif.parse(t"a*").subsumes(motif)   // false
      ```

      Any input type with a `Symbolizer` can be matched; `Text` is the one provided. A motif
      made from an `r"…"` literal under `regexBackends.re2` is staged into the program at
      compile time through `.staged`, so its `#node` is `Node.Empty`: only the program takes
      part in matching.
  refer praxinoscope.Program
  refer praxinoscope.Ranges
  refer praxinoscope.Subsumption
  refer kaleidoscope.Regex
  entry .parse   parses RE2 syntax into a `Motif`, or raises `Motif.Error` at the offending position
      A recursive-descent parser for the RE2 syntax listed on the `praxinoscope` package:
      character classes are resolved to `Ranges` as it goes (so a negated class and its
      complement are the same node), capturing groups are numbered from 1 in opening order,
      the inline flags are scoped to the enclosing group, and the node tree is compiled to a
      `Program` at the end. Needs a `Tactic[Motif.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Motif.parse(t"a(b|c)d").node
      // Sequence(List(Literal('a'), Group(Alternation(List(Literal('b'), Literal('c'))), 1), Literal('d')))

      Motif.parse(t"(?i)abc").matches(t"AbC")    // true
      Motif.parse(t"\\p{Greek}+").matches(t"αβ")  // true
      Motif.parse(t"[[:^digit:]]").matches(t"a") // true
      ```

      What is rejected, and why: a backreference `\1` or `\k<name>` (`Backreference`),
      lookaround `(?=…)`, `(?!…)`, `(?<=…)`, `(?<!…)` (`Lookaround`), a possessive `*+`
      (`PossessiveQuantifier`), an atomic group `(?>…)` (`AtomicGroup`), a flag letter other
      than `i`, `m`, `s`, `U` (`Flag`), a repetition count over 1000 (`RepetitionTooLarge`),
      an escape of a letter with no meaning (`InvalidEscape`), and the structural faults an
      unclosed group, class or repetition, a stray `)`, an empty `[]` or an inverted `[z-a]`.
      A `{` not followed by a digit is a literal, as RE2 reads it, so `a{x}` matches those
      four characters.
  entry .apply   `Motif(text)`: the same as `.parse`
  entry .staged   rebuilds a motif compiled at compile time, at most once per pattern
      `Motif.staged(pattern, captures, program)` is what a staged motif expands to: the
      program is by-name and is materialised only on the first call for a pattern, then
      served from a cache keyed by the pattern text. A `case r"…" =>` under
      `regexBackends.re2` constructs its extractor on every match, so without the cache the
      staged tables would be rebuilt each time. A staged motif's `node` is `Node.Empty`.
  entry .maxRepetition   the largest count a `{n,m}` quantifier may name: 1000, as RE2 sets it
      Bounded repetitions compile by expansion, so the cap bounds program size; exceeding it
      is `Motif.Error.Reason.RepetitionTooLarge`.
  entry .Node   the structural form of a parsed pattern
    detail
        An enum of eight cases. `Empty` matches the empty string; `Literal(codepoint)` one
        codepoint; `Klass(ranges)` any codepoint in a `Ranges` (every class form — `[…]`,
        `.`, `\d`, `\p{…}`, `[[:…:]]` — becomes one, negation already applied); `Sequence(nodes)`
        and `Alternation(options)` concatenate and choose; `Repeat(child, minimum,
        maximum, reluctant)` is any quantifier, with `maximum` `Unset` for unbounded;
        `Group(child, index)` is a group whose `index` is its 1-based capture number, or
        `Unset` for a non-capturing one; and `Boundary(anchor)` is a zero-width test, one of
        `Node.Anchor`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Motif.parse(t"(ab)+").node
        // Repeat(Group(Sequence(List(Literal('a'), Literal('b'))), 1), 1, Unset, false)
        ```
    entry .Anchor   the zero-width tests: `Start`, `End`, `WordBoundary`, `NonWordBoundary`, `LineStart`, `LineEnd`
        `Start` is `^` or `\A` and `End` is `$` or `\z`, the ends of the whole input;
        `WordBoundary` is `\b` and `NonWordBoundary` `\B`, where a word character (`\w`,
        ASCII) meets a non-word one or not; `LineStart` and `LineEnd` are `^` and `$` under
        the `m` flag, which also match beside a newline. The containment analyses cannot
        model the last four and raise `Unverifiable` for a program containing one.
  entry .Error   raised when a pattern is not RE2 syntax, or an analysis cannot answer
    detail
        Error 500: _the regular expression could not be compiled because `reason` at
        `index`_, with the reason's number as the sub-number. Raised by `Motif.parse` through
        a `Tactic[Motif.Error]` at the offset of the offending character; by `Program.compile`
        when a program exceeds `Program.maxOps`; by `Subsumption` (and so `Motif#subsumes`,
        `Motif#intersects` and kaleidoscope's `Regex#subsumes`) for a pattern with a word or
        line boundary, or an exploration past `Subsumption.maxStates`; and by kaleidoscope's
        `Regex#re2`, which parses a regex for this engine as it retags it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        safely(Motif.parse(t"(?=a)"))   // Unset: Motif.Error(0, Lookaround)
        ```
    entry .Reason   why a pattern was rejected, as one of twenty-one numbered cases
      detail
          The structural faults: `UnclosedGroup` (1), `NotInGroup` (2) for a stray `)`,
          `UnexpectedChar` (3) for a misplaced quantifier or a malformed repetition,
          `BadRepetition` (4) for `{3,1}`, `IncompleteRepetition` (5), `EmptyCharClass` (6),
          `UnclosedClass` (7), `UnclosedEscape` (8), `InvalidEscape` (9) for an escaped letter
          with no meaning or a bad `\x` form, `InvertedRange` (10) for `[z-a]`,
          `InvalidPosixClass` (20) and `UnknownUnicodeClass` (21). The constructs RE2 leaves
          out: `Backreference` (11), `Lookaround` (12), `PossessiveQuantifier` (13),
          `AtomicGroup` (14), `UnsupportedGroup` (15) for a `(?P` not followed by `<`, and
          `Flag` (16) for a flag letter other than `i`, `m`, `s`, `U`. The limits:
          `RepetitionTooLarge` (17) for a count over `Motif.maxRepetition` or a program over
          `Program.maxOps`, `BudgetExceeded` (18) for a containment analysis past
          `Subsumption.maxStates`, and `Unverifiable` (19) for a containment question about a
          pattern with a word or line boundary. Each case's `number` is the error's sub-number.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message: _backreferences are not part of RE2 syntax_, …
      entry #number   the reason's number, 1 to 21, which is the sub-number of its `Motif.Error`
    entry #index   the offset in the pattern at which the fault was found; 0 for an analysis failure
    entry #reason   the `Motif.Error.Reason`
  entry #pattern   the pattern as parsed
  entry #node   the structural tree, a `Motif.Node`; `Node.Empty` for a motif staged at compile time
  entry #captures   the number of capturing groups
  entry #program   the compiled `Program` the Pike VM runs
  entry #matches   whether the whole input matches
      A whole-input test, anchored at both ends: `Motif.parse(t"abc").matches(t"xabcx")` is
      false. Takes any input with a `Symbolizer`; `Text` is provided. Linear in the input.
  entry #seek   the interval of the leftmost match at or after `start`, or `Unset`
      Leftmost-first: the match a backtracking engine would find first. `start` is an
      `Ordinal`, `Prim` by default, and the `Interval` is zero-based over UTF-16 units. A
      pattern which must begin with a literal is sought by jumping between occurrences of
      that literal (`Program#prefix`), and one which must begin with some class of symbol by
      scanning for one; a nullable pattern matches emptily at `start`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Motif.parse(t"\\bfoo\\b").seek(t"afoo foo")   // Interval.zerary(5, 8)
      Motif.parse(t"z").seek(t"foo")               // Unset
      ```
  entry #search   every match at or after `start`, as a lazily-built `Chain` of intervals
      Each search resumes at the end of the previous match, or one past it when the match
      was empty, so `a*` over `ab` yields the intervals 0–1, 1–1 and 2–2. Kaleidoscope's
      `Regex#search` on a `Regex in Re2` differs in resuming one past every match's end, to
      agree with `java.util.regex`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Motif.parse(t"a").search(t"banana")
      // Chain(Interval.zerary(1, 2), Interval.zerary(3, 4), Interval.zerary(5, 6))
      ```
  entry #subsumes   whether this motif matches every input another does, or every input a list of others all do
      `motif.subsumes(that)` decides `L(that) ⊆ L(this)`; `motif.subsumes(those)` decides
      `L(⋂those) ⊆ L(this)`, containment of the inputs matched by *all* of a list of motifs at
      once, which no single RE2 pattern can express (RE2 has no intersection operator) and
      which a pairwise check gets wrong. Both are `Subsumption.subsumes` over the compiled
      programs, and raise `Motif.Error` for a word or line boundary in any pattern
      (`Unverifiable`) or a pair which exhausts the budget (`BudgetExceeded`). Equivalent
      patterns subsume each other, so two checks decide equivalence.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def motif(text: Text): Motif = Motif.parse(text)

      motif(t"[a-z]+").subsumes(motif(t"abc|def"))   // true
      motif(t"a").subsumes(motif(t"^a$$"))           // true: a redundant anchor changes nothing

      motif(t"[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{4}").subsumes
       (List(motif(t"[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{4}|[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{2}"),
             motif(t"[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{4}|[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{6}")))   // true, though neither alone is subsumed
      ```
  entry #intersects   whether some input matches both this motif and another
      `Subsumption.intersects` over the two programs: a polynomial reachability question,
      unlike `#subsumes`. Two nullable patterns intersect on the empty input. Raises
      `Motif.Error` for a word or line boundary in either.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Motif.parse(t"[a-m]+").intersects(Motif.parse(t"[n-z]+"))   // false
      Motif.parse(t"a*").intersects(Motif.parse(t"b*"))           // true
      ```
  entry #groups   what each capturing group matched in a whole-input match, in group order
      `Optional[List[Optional[Interval]]]`: `Unset` if the input does not match, and within
      the list `Unset` for a group which took part in no iteration. A repeated group records
      its last iteration; a greedy group captures maximally and a reluctant one minimally.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Motif.parse(t"(a)?b").groups(t"b")     // List(Unset)
      Motif.parse(t"(a|b)+").groups(t"ab")   // List(Interval.zerary(1, 2))
      ```
  entry #spans   as `#groups`, with the whole match at index 0, and optionally unanchored from `start`
      With `anchored` true (the default) the whole input must match; with it false, the
      leftmost match from `start` is reported, as `#seek` finds it.
  entry #slots   the raw capture-slot array of a match: group `n`'s bounds at `2n` and `2n + 1`
      The winning thread's slots as an `Array[Int]`, with the whole match at 0 and 1 and
      `-1` for a slot no thread reached; `Unset` for no match. It exists beside `#spans`
      because an `Interval` cannot record the *position* of an empty match, which a caller
      stepping through repeated matches needs. `anchored` false seeks from `start` instead of
      requiring the whole input.

entry .Program   a pattern compiled to a flat instruction array, for the Pike VM and the containment analyses
  detail
      A `Program` is the `#ops` of a pattern as an array of `Program.Op`, with capture group
      `n` recording its bounds in slots `2n` and `2n + 1` and the whole match in slots 0 and
      1, so `#slots` is `2 × (captures + 1)`. `.compile` builds one from a `Motif.Node`, and
      `Motif#program` holds it; `Pike.run` executes it and `Subsumption` analyses it. The
      lazily computed `#nullable`, `#firstBounds` and `#prefix` are what let an unanchored
      seek skip positions at which no match can begin.
  refer praxinoscope.Motif
  refer praxinoscope.Pike
  entry .compile   compiles a node tree to a program, raising `Motif.Error` if it grows past `.maxOps`
      Emits one `Symbol` op per literal or class, `Split`s for alternation and repetition
      (preferred branch first, which is how greed and reluctance are encoded), `Jump`s for
      loops, `Save`s around a capturing group, `Test`s for anchors, and a final `Accept`. A
      bounded repetition `{n,m}` is expanded, so nested bounded repetitions grow
      geometrically; past `maxOps` ops the compilation raises
      `Motif.Error.Reason.RepetitionTooLarge`.
  entry .maxOps   the ceiling on a program's length: 100000 ops
  entry .Op   one instruction: `Symbol`, `Split`, `Jump`, `Save`, `Test` or `Accept`
      `Symbol(bounds, next)` consumes one symbol within the interleaved `lo, hi, lo, hi, …`
      bounds (a `Ranges` flattened for allocation-free scanning) and continues at `next`;
      `Split(preferred, alternate)` forks a thread, preferring the first; `Jump(target)`
      continues elsewhere; `Save(slot, next)` records the current position in a capture slot;
      `Test(anchor, next)` passes only where the `Motif.Node.Anchor` holds; `Accept` ends a
      match. Only `Symbol` consumes input; the rest are followed at no cost in the VM's
      ε-closure.
  entry #ops   the instructions, indexed by program counter
  entry #slots   the number of capture slots: two per group, plus two for the whole match
  entry #captures   the number of capturing groups
  entry #entry   the program counter execution starts at: 0
  entry #nullable   whether a match may consume nothing, in which case no position can be skipped when seeking
  entry #firstBounds   the interleaved bounds of every symbol a match can begin with, coalesced
      Empty when no symbol can begin a match. An unanchored seek over a pattern with no
      literal prefix scans for a symbol within these bounds before starting threads.
  entry #prefix   the longest literal every match must begin with, or empty text
      Capped at 32 UTF-16 units, and empty when the first consuming op is not a single
      unique symbol. An unanchored seek jumps between occurrences of it with
      `Symbolizer#find`, which for `Text` is `String.indexOf`, rather than stepping through
      every position.

entry .Pike   the virtual machine which runs a `Program` over an input in linear time
  detail
      A Pike VM simulates the automaton breadth-first: at each input position it holds a
      priority-ordered list of threads, one per program counter, advances every thread over
      the symbol at once, and never backtracks. Each program counter joins a position's
      thread list at most once, so a run costs at most the input length times the program
      length, whatever the pattern, which is the guarantee the whole library rests on. Thread
      order is preference order, so the first thread to accept is the match a backtracking
      engine would have found first, with the same capture bounds.
  refer praxinoscope.Program
  refer praxinoscope.Motif
  entry .run   runs a program from a UTF-16 offset, returning the winning thread's capture slots or `Unset`
      `Pike.run(program, input, start, anchoredStart, anchoredEnd)`, over any input with a
      `Symbolizer`. With `anchoredStart` false, a fresh lowest-priority thread joins at every
      position until a match is found — leftmost-first `seek` semantics — and positions at
      which no match can begin are skipped by `Program#prefix` or `Program#firstBounds`;
      with `anchoredEnd` true, the match must reach the end of the input. The slots are an
      `Array[Int]` with `-1` where no thread saved a position. `Motif#matches`, `#seek`,
      `#search` and `#slots` are the usual callers.

entry .Ranges   a set of symbols as sorted, disjoint runs of codepoints: the form every character class takes
  detail
      A `Ranges` holds its `#spans` as an interleaved list of inclusive bounds, `lo :: hi ::
      lo :: hi :: …`, sorted, disjoint and never adjacent, so two sets are equal exactly when
      their spans are. The constructor is private; `.apply`, `.point`, `.empty` and the named
      classes build normalised sets, and `#union`, `#negate`, `#intersect` and `#folded`
      preserve the invariant. Symbols are `Int`-encoded members of an ordered alphabet,
      Unicode codepoints up to `.maxSymbol` today.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Ranges(0, 10).union(Ranges(5, 15)).spans     // List(0, 15)
      Ranges(5, 9).negate().spans                   // List(0, 4, 10, 0x10ffff)
      Ranges(0, 10).intersect(Ranges(5, 15)).spans  // List(5, 10)
      Ranges.digit.contains('7')                    // true
      Ranges.posix.at("alpha")                      // letters A–Z and a–z
      Ranges.unicode("Lu")                          // every uppercase letter
      ```

      The parser resolves every class form to a `Ranges`, so `Motif.parse(t"[a-cd-f]").node`
      is `Klass(Ranges('a', 'f'))`, the adjacent runs coalesced.
  refer praxinoscope.Motif
  entry .maxSymbol   the greatest symbol in the Unicode codepoint domain: `0x10ffff`
      The upper bound `#negate` complements against. Any other symbol domain is smaller, so it
      is a safe universal bound.
  entry .empty   the set with no symbols
  entry .apply   `Ranges(lo, hi)`: the inclusive run from `lo` to `hi`
  entry .point   `Ranges.point(symbol)`: the set of one symbol
  entry .digit   `\d`: the ASCII digits `0`–`9`
      RE2's perl classes are ASCII-only, as are `.word` and `.space`.
  entry .word   `\w`: ASCII letters, digits and `_`
  entry .space   `\s`: tab, newline, vertical tab, form feed, carriage return and space
  entry .any   `.`: every codepoint but newline
      Under the `s` flag, `.` is `.anySymbol` instead.
  entry .anySymbol   every codepoint: `\p{Any}`, and `.` under the `s` flag
  entry .where   every symbol a predicate admits, as coalesced runs
      Scans the whole codepoint domain once, which is how the Unicode classes are made exact
      without a second copy of the Unicode database: the JDK's own `Character` tables answer
      the predicate. One pass per distinct class, which `.unicode` memoises.
  entry .posix   the POSIX classes of `[[:name:]]`, by name, all ASCII-only
      `alnum`, `alpha`, `ascii`, `blank`, `cntrl`, `digit`, `graph`, `lower`, `print`,
      `punct`, `space`, `upper`, `word` and `xdigit`, as a `Map[String, Ranges]`; a name
      not present is `Motif.Error.Reason.InvalidPosixClass` when parsed.
  entry .unicode   a Unicode general category or script by the name `\p{…}` uses, or `Unset`
      `Any` is the whole domain; a two-letter category such as `Lu` or `Nd` is matched
      exactly; a one-letter group such as `L` is the union of its subcategories, as RE2
      defines it; and any other name is tried as a script, `Greek` or `Han`. The result is
      memoised, since each class costs one pass over the domain. An unknown name is
      `Motif.Error.Reason.UnknownUnicodeClass` when parsed.
  entry #spans   the interleaved inclusive bounds, `lo :: hi :: lo :: hi :: …`
  entry #vacant   whether the set is empty
  entry #contains   whether a symbol is in the set
  entry #union   the symbols in either set, coalesced
  entry #negate   the symbols not in the set, up to `max` (`Ranges.maxSymbol` by default)
  entry #intersect   the symbols in both sets
  entry #folded   the closure of the set under simple case folding, as the `i` flag defines it
      A fold orbit is not always a pair — `K`, `k` and the Kelvin sign fold together — so
      the closure is computed over orbits rather than by mapping each symbol up and down.
      Only the case-varying codepoints are examined, so the cost is proportional to their
      number rather than to the set's size.

entry .Subsumption   decides containment and intersection of the languages of compiled programs
  detail
      Two questions a backtracking engine cannot answer, decidable here because RE2 patterns
      are regular languages: `.subsumes`, whether every input one program matches another
      also matches (worst-case exponential: the covering program is determinised on the fly,
      so the exploration is budgeted by `.maxStates`), and `.intersects`, whether some input
      matches both (polynomial). `Motif#subsumes`, `Motif#intersects` and kaleidoscope's
      `Regex#subsumes` and `Regex#intersects` are the usual way to ask them. Word and line
      boundaries are context-dependent in a way the analyses do not model, so a program
      containing one raises `Motif.Error.Reason.Unverifiable`.
  refer praxinoscope.Motif
  refer praxinoscope.Program
  entry .maxStates   the budget on explored states: 100000, past which `.subsumes` raises `BudgetExceeded`
  entry .subsumes   whether every input matched by one program, or by all of a list, is matched by the cover
      `Subsumption.subsumes(first, second)` decides `L(second) ⊆ L(first)`;
      `Subsumption.subsumes(cover, candidates)` decides `L(⋂candidates) ⊆ L(cover)`: each
      candidate is explored program counter by program counter while the cover is
      determinised, and containment fails at a reachable state which every candidate accepts
      and the cover does not. An empty candidate list answers `false`, failing closed, rather
      than deciding whether the cover matches everything. Raises `Motif.Error` for
      `Unverifiable` or `BudgetExceeded`.
  entry .intersects   whether some input is matched by both programs
      Reachability, in the product of the two automata, of a point where both accept.
      Raises `Motif.Error` for `Unverifiable` or `BudgetExceeded`.

entry .Symbolizer   how an input is read as successive `Int`-encoded symbols, so a program can run over it
  detail
      A typeclass, `input is Symbolizer`, which decodes an input into the symbols a `Program`
      consumes: its `#length`, the `#symbol` at a UTF-16 offset, the offset of the `#next`
      symbol, the symbol `#before` an offset, whether a symbol is a `#word` character, and
      `#find`, which locates a literal for seek acceleration. The program representation
      depends on nothing else about the input, so a UTF-8 byte domain, for streamed or native
      matching, could be added without changing `Program` or `Pike`. `.text` is the one
      instance provided, over `Text` as Unicode codepoints, in implicit scope.
  refer praxinoscope.Pike
  entry .text   `Text is Symbolizer`: Unicode codepoints over UTF-16 text
      `codePointAt` and `codePointBefore`, so an astral character is one symbol; `find` is
      `String.indexOf`, which the JVM intrinsifies into a vectorised scan.
  entry #Self   the input type
  entry #length   the input's length in offsets
  entry #symbol   the symbol at an offset
  entry #next   the offset after the symbol at an offset
  entry #before   the symbol ending at an offset
  entry #word   whether a symbol is a word character (`\w`, ASCII), for `\b` and `\B`
  entry #find   the offset of the first occurrence of a literal at or after `from`, or negative if none

entry .Fsa   a whole-input matcher generated as the code compiles: a static automaton for one pattern
  detail
      An `Fsa` wraps a `#test` from `Text` to `Boolean`, generated by subset construction for
      a captureless `r"…"` literal under `regexBackends.re2`: a dense transition table over
      the pattern's symbol classes, driven by an allocation-free loop which neither
      backtracks nor tracks threads. It is a named holder rather than a bare function type so
      that capture-checked consumers can carry one through quoted code. The generator
      declines — and the literal falls back to its staged `Program` — for a pattern with a
      word or line boundary, or one whose automaton exceeds 512 states. A program does not
      build one by hand.
  refer kaleidoscope.regexBackends
  entry .of   the matcher for a pattern, built at most once
      `Fsa.of(key)(fsa)`: `fsa` is by-name and is evaluated only the first time `key` — the
      pattern's string literal — is seen, since an extractor is constructed on every match.
  entry #test   the generated whole-input test
  entry #matches   whether the whole text matches

entry .internal   the staging of motifs and programs into compiled code, and the static matcher generator
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the `ToExpr` givens which let kaleidoscope's `r"…"` macro splice a
      `Motif` (as a `Motif.staged` call), a `Program`, each `Program.Op` and each
      `Motif.Node.Anchor` into the extractor it emits, and `matcher`, which compiles a program
      by subset construction to the `Fsa` a captureless literal uses.
  entry .anchor   `ToExpr[Motif.Node.Anchor]`
  entry .op   `ToExpr[Program.Op]`
  entry .program   `ToExpr[Program]`: the ops as an array literal
  entry .motif   `ToExpr[Motif]`: a `Motif.staged(pattern, captures, program)` call, cached by pattern
  entry .maxStates   the cap on a generated automaton: 512 states, above which `.matcher` declines
  entry .matcher   generates an `Fsa` expression for a program, or `Unset`
      Subset construction to a dense table over the alphabet's equivalence classes, with a
      direct-indexed class map for ASCII and a binary search above; `Unset` for a program
      with a word or line boundary, or one whose state count exceeds `.maxStates`.
